structure,information, and identify potential data quality issues. Duplicate records were removed, and the target variable, deposit, was separated from the input features. The target values were converted into numerical labels, where 0 represented no subscription and 1 represented subscription.\n","\n","The duration feature was excluded because it represents the length of a marketing call and would not be available when making a prediction before the call. Numerical features were standardised using StandardScaler, while categorical features were converted into numerical representations using OneHotEncoder. Finally, the dataset was divided into 80% training data and 20% testing data, using stratified sampling to maintain the class distribution.\n","\n","Logistic Regression was selected as the classification algorithm because it is suitable for binary classification problems. It was trained using the preproccessed training data. After training and making predictions, the model was then evaluated using accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, a confusion matrix and the ROC AUC score.\n","\n","The model achieved an accuracy of 69.55%, meaning approximately 70% of its predictions were correct. It achieved a precision of 73.28%, indicating that when the model predicted a customer would subscribe, approximately 73% of those predictions were correct. However, its recall was lower at 56.24%, meaning it identified only around 56% of the customers who actually subscribed. The F1-score was 63.64%, showing a moderate balance between precision and recall. The ROC AUC score was 0.7579, suggesting that the model has a reasonable ability to distinguish between subscribers and non-subscribers."]}],"metadata":{"kaggle":{"accelerator":"none","dataSources":[{"sourceType":"datasetVersion","sourceId":22660275}],"isInternetEnabled":false,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":false},"kernelspec":{"display_name":"Python 3","language":"python","name":"python3"},"language_in